    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d545">

                <pb facs="0641" n="573" sameAs="frus:frus1914-20v01/pg573"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.857 M 331/42</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to President Wilson </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-22">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 22, 1916</hi>.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. President</hi>: I enclose the report of
                    our Consul at Cork, Mr. Frost, dated November 1st,<anchor n="65"
                        xml:id="d545-f1"/> in the case of the <hi rendition="#italic">Marina</hi>,
                    to which is attached the affidavits of the American citizens who were <pb
                        facs="0642" n="574" xml:id="pg574"/> on board the vessel. I also enclose the
                    report of the Consul dated November 2d on the same subject.<anchor n="66"
                        xml:id="d545-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>It seems to me to be a case in which, from the facts sworn to, there appears to
                    be little possible defense. Even the excuse that the vessel was laden with
                    contraband for Great Britain is absent, in the fact that she was on her homeward
                    voyage and practically in ballast.</p>
                <p>I have been considering in my own mind whether it is possible for us to avoid
                    taking definite action in the case in accordance with our <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi> note. I do not feel, however, that I wish to
                    express an opinion at present, although I consider the situation very grave.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
